1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,550
Images, and documents.

2
00:00:04,350 --> 00:00:08,099
Six episodes into building a design studio's website,

3
00:00:08,099 --> 00:00:10,434
and there is not one image on it.

4
00:00:10,434 --> 00:00:11,707
Today we fix that.

5
00:00:11,707 --> 00:00:16,943
And this is the first episode where the thing we make does not live in the

6
00:00:16,943 --> 00:00:18,216
repository at all.

7
00:00:19,016 --> 00:00:21,336
And there is nothing to install.

8
00:00:21,336 --> 00:00:24,381
We toured all of this back in episode two.

9
00:00:24,381 --> 00:00:30,327
The images app and the documents app have been sitting in installed apps since the

10
00:00:30,327 --> 00:00:31,269
first minute.

11
00:00:31,269 --> 00:00:34,822
Media root and media URL are already in settings.

12
00:00:34,822 --> 00:00:40,042
The URL config already serves uploaded files, but only when debug is on.

13
00:00:40,042 --> 00:00:42,290
Production is episode thirteen.

14
00:00:43,090 --> 00:00:45,622
And this is worth being clear about.

15
00:00:45,622 --> 00:00:47,801
Your repository holds the site.

16
00:00:47,801 --> 00:00:51,528
The database and the media folder hold what is in it.

17
00:00:51,528 --> 00:00:56,943
Uploading an image is not a code change, which is why media is in git ignore,

18
00:00:56,943 --> 00:01:02,428
and why the images in this episode will not arrive when you check out the tag.

19
00:01:03,228 --> 00:01:06,956
An image on a page is just a Django foreign key.

20
00:01:06,956 --> 00:01:09,441
There is no special image field.

21
00:01:09,441 --> 00:01:14,334
But three of these arguments matter, and people get them wrong.

22
00:01:15,134 --> 00:01:20,872
Cascade would mean that deleting an image in the admin deletes every page that used

23
00:01:20,872 --> 00:01:21,080
it.

24
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:25,781
An editor tidying up the image library could take out half the site.

25
00:01:25,781 --> 00:01:31,312
Set null means the page just loses its hero, which is what anybody would expect.

26
00:01:31,312 --> 00:01:36,704
And related name plus, because we never ask an image which pages are using it.

27
00:01:37,504 --> 00:01:40,532
So what does the image tag actually do.

28
00:01:40,532 --> 00:01:43,948
It does not resize anything on each request.

29
00:01:43,948 --> 00:01:49,305
The first time, it generates a copy, writes it into the media folder,

30
00:01:49,305 --> 00:01:52,488
and records a row in the rendition table.

31
00:01:52,488 --> 00:01:55,283
Every time after that, the row wins.

32
00:01:55,283 --> 00:01:58,543
And your original upload is never touched.

33
00:01:58,543 --> 00:02:00,562
Every rendition is a copy.

34
00:02:01,362 --> 00:02:04,154
There are a handful of filter specs.

35
00:02:04,154 --> 00:02:05,162
Width scales.

36
00:02:05,162 --> 00:02:08,651
Fill crops to exactly the size you asked for.

37
00:02:08,651 --> 00:02:14,390
Max fits the image inside a box without cropping and without enlarging it.

38
00:02:14,390 --> 00:02:18,732
The one to remember is that fill crops and max does not.

39
00:02:19,532 --> 00:02:22,438
And responsive images are one tag.

40
00:02:22,438 --> 00:02:27,224
Srcset image takes a list of specs, generates all three,

41
00:02:27,224 --> 00:02:31,582
and writes out a proper srcset and sizes attribute.

42
00:02:31,582 --> 00:02:38,248
I checked it at 375 pixels wide, and the browser downloads the 800-pixel file,

43
00:02:38,248 --> 00:02:39,359
not the 1600.

44
00:02:39,359 --> 00:02:43,718
Your phone visitors stop paying for desktop images.

45
00:02:44,518 --> 00:02:45,547
Now, cropping.

46
00:02:45,547 --> 00:02:51,573
These are two renditions of exactly the same image, at exactly the same fill spec.

47
00:02:51,573 --> 00:02:57,598
On the left, Wagtail crops from the centre and slices the subject off at the edge.

48
00:02:57,598 --> 00:03:01,420
On the right, the same crop, with a focal point set.

49
00:03:01,420 --> 00:03:06,784
That is the whole feature, and you set it by dragging a box in the admin.

50
00:03:07,584 --> 00:03:08,500
Two details.

51
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:12,698
First, the filename changes when you set a focal point.

52
00:03:12,698 --> 00:03:18,193
That hash is part of the cache key, so a stale crop can never be served.

53
00:03:18,193 --> 00:03:21,399
And second, focal points only affect fill.

54
00:03:21,399 --> 00:03:27,810
Width and max do not crop anything, so there is nothing for a focal point to decide.

55
00:03:28,610 --> 00:03:32,568
And here is the one that will catch you in production.

56
00:03:32,568 --> 00:03:35,572
Look at what get rendition actually does.

57
00:03:35,572 --> 00:03:37,624
It looks for a database row.

58
00:03:37,624 --> 00:03:39,896
If it finds one, it returns it.

59
00:03:39,896 --> 00:03:44,660
It never checks whether the file that row points at still exists.

60
00:03:45,460 --> 00:03:49,505
So I cleared out the rendition files and audited it.

61
00:03:49,505 --> 00:03:54,873
Fourteen rows, eleven of them pointing at files that no longer exist.

62
00:03:54,873 --> 00:03:59,618
And the site cheerfully puts all eleven URLs into image tags,

63
00:03:59,618 --> 00:04:01,952
and every single one is a 404.

64
00:04:01,952 --> 00:04:04,596
Nothing in the logs tells you why.

65
00:04:04,596 --> 00:04:06,308
This is not contrived.

66
00:04:06,308 --> 00:04:11,598
It is what happens after a media restore, or a move to a new server,

67
00:04:11,598 --> 00:04:14,398
or somebody clearing out disk space.

68
00:04:15,198 --> 00:04:17,612
And the fix ships with Wagtail.

69
00:04:17,612 --> 00:04:22,750
Purge the rendition rows, and they regenerate on the next request.

70
00:04:22,750 --> 00:04:28,745
After that the audit came back clean: nine rows, nine files, nothing missing.

71
00:04:28,745 --> 00:04:31,704
Put this one in your deployment notes.

72
00:04:32,504 --> 00:04:35,853
Now something that matters more than it looks.

73
00:04:35,853 --> 00:04:39,420
If you write the image tag with no alt attribute,

74
00:04:39,420 --> 00:04:42,331
Wagtail falls back to the image's title.

75
00:04:42,331 --> 00:04:45,243
Ours came out as alt equals Studio hero.

76
00:04:45,243 --> 00:04:48,301
That is how the image is filed in the CMS.

77
00:04:48,301 --> 00:04:53,906
It tells somebody using a screen reader absolutely nothing about the picture.

78
00:04:54,706 --> 00:04:59,337
In a StreamField, the answer is image block, not image chooser block.

79
00:04:59,337 --> 00:05:01,887
It arrived in Wagtail six point three,

80
00:05:01,887 --> 00:05:07,390
and it carries alt text and a decorative checkbox right next to the image chooser,

81
00:05:07,390 --> 00:05:11,416
so the person writing the page is the one who supplies them.

82
00:05:11,416 --> 00:05:16,852
It still behaves as an image everywhere the old block did, so switching is cheap.

83
00:05:17,652 --> 00:05:19,295
And here is the opinion.

84
00:05:19,295 --> 00:05:24,979
If your CMS makes alt text optional and invisible, you will ship a site without it.

85
00:05:24,979 --> 00:05:30,183
On the hero, which is a plain foreign key, there is nothing to inherit from,

86
00:05:30,183 --> 00:05:35,866
so we added our own hero alt field right next to the image chooser where nobody can

87
00:05:35,866 --> 00:05:36,414
miss it.

88
00:05:37,214 --> 00:05:41,127
Documents work the same way, with a document chooser block.

89
00:05:41,127 --> 00:05:44,973
And the size and file type come straight off the document,

90
00:05:44,973 --> 00:05:49,880
so the visitor knows what they are about to download before they click it.

91
00:05:50,680 --> 00:05:55,557
One thing to notice: that document URL is not a static file path.

92
00:05:55,557 --> 00:05:58,484
Documents are served by a Wagtail view,

93
00:05:58,484 --> 00:06:04,636
which is exactly what makes private documents and permission checks possible later

94
00:06:04,636 --> 00:06:04,862
on.

95
00:06:04,862 --> 00:06:06,437
Images are different.

96
00:06:06,437 --> 00:06:10,114
Those really are plain files sitting under/media.

97
00:06:10,914 --> 00:06:13,871
One warning before we look at the result.

98
00:06:13,871 --> 00:06:18,342
There is a setting that lets you swap in your own image model,

99
00:06:18,342 --> 00:06:22,020
with extra fields like a photo credit or a licence.

100
00:06:22,020 --> 00:06:25,842
It is genuinely useful, and it is a day one decision.

101
00:06:25,842 --> 00:06:31,611
Retrofitting it onto a site that already has content means migrating every image

102
00:06:31,611 --> 00:06:33,559
foreign key in the project.

103
00:06:33,559 --> 00:06:34,640
Know it exists.

104
00:06:34,640 --> 00:06:36,948
Do not do it to this site today.

105
00:06:37,748 --> 00:06:39,535
And here is the result.

106
00:06:39,535 --> 00:06:42,721
A hero above the title, with its caption,

107
00:06:42,721 --> 00:06:48,470
cropped to a banner from an image nearly twice as wide as it needed to be.

108
00:06:49,270 --> 00:06:52,852
And further down, the image block set to full width.

109
00:06:52,852 --> 00:06:58,019
Look at where it ends compared to the quote and the download underneath it.

110
00:06:58,019 --> 00:07:01,120
It genuinely breaks out past the text column.

111
00:07:01,120 --> 00:07:03,393
Its caption came from the editor,

112
00:07:03,393 --> 00:07:08,560
and the download shows its file type and its size before anybody clicks it.

113
00:07:09,360 --> 00:07:10,610
And the About page.

114
00:07:10,610 --> 00:07:14,754
That is a tall portrait image being cropped into a wide banner,

115
00:07:14,754 --> 00:07:17,517
and the face is still in the middle of it,

116
00:07:17,517 --> 00:07:22,122
because the focal point tells Wagtail what the picture is actually of.

117
00:07:22,922 --> 00:07:24,294
Commit, and tag.

118
00:07:24,294 --> 00:07:28,412
Remember the images themselves are not in there.

119
00:07:29,212 --> 00:07:31,032
Next episode, the blog.

120
00:07:31,032 --> 00:07:37,523
An index page with posts underneath it, restricting which page types can go where,

121
00:07:37,523 --> 00:07:38,710
and pagination.

122
00:07:39,510 --> 00:07:44,431
Next episode, the blog.

123
00:07:44,431 --> 00:07:48,710
Thanks for watching.
